    "last_assistant_text": "So `IsAnycastRoutingLocator()` checks `m8[14] == 0xfc`. For kAloc16Leader = 0xfc00, `m8[14]` (= high byte of m16[7] in network order) = 0xfc. So the Leader ALOC IS an anycast routing locator.\n\nNow let me look at `HandleDatagram` from `SendRaw`:\n- `aFromNcpHost = true` \n- Line 1179: `IsUnicastAddress(header.GetDestination())` - is the Leader ALOC registered?\n\nLooking at `AddLeaderAloc()`:\n```cpp\notError Mle::AddLeaderAloc(void)\n{\n    VerifyOrExit(mRole == OT_DEVICE_ROLE_LEADER, error = OT_ERROR_INVALID_STATE);\n    SuccessOrExit(error = GetLeaderAloc(mLeaderAloc.GetAddress()));\n    error = Get<ThreadNetif>().AddUnicastAddress(mLeaderAloc);\n}\n```\n\nThis adds the Leader ALOC as a unicast address. So `IsUnicastAddress` should return true for it.\n\nBut wait - maybe there's an issue with how `ProcessReceiveCallback` works for packets from the NCP host. Let me re-read the flow:\n\n```cpp\nif (receive)\n{\n    ...\n    ProcessReceiveCallback(aMessage, messageInfo, nextHeader, aFromNcpHost);\n    SuccessOrExit(error = HandlePayload(aMessage, messageInfo, nextHeader));\n}\n```\n\n`ProcessReceiveCallback` with `aFromNcpHost=true` returns early (line 1005: `VerifyOrExit(!aFromNcpHost, error = OT_ERROR_NO_ROUTE)`), which is fine because it just skips the receive callback and continues. Then `HandlePayload` processes the UDP, which calls the CoAP handler.\n\nHmm, the code looks correct. Let me try a slightly different approach - maybe the issue is with `HandleExtensionHeaders` at line 1197:",
